verifier.better_fix:
Don't change normal_pos. Fix the motion so that it steps over and lands on whole graphemes, in motions.rs word_end:
```rust
fn word_end(t: &TextBuf, p: usize, big: bool) -> Option<usize> {
    // Past the whole grapheme under the cursor, which may carry combining marks.
    let mut c = Chars::at(t, next_stop(t, p)?);
    loop {
        match class(c.ch()?, big) {
            Class::Blank => {}
            Class::Newline if empty_line(&c) => return Some(c.pos()),
            Class::Newline => {}
            k => loop {
                let here = c.pos();
                c.fwd();
                if c.ch().map(|ch| class(ch, big)) != Some(k) {
                    // On the grapheme holding the word's last character, as in Vim.
                    return Some(t.snap(here, Some(Dir::Backward)));
                }
            },
        }
        c.fwd();
    }
}
```
Tested on main: `e` gives 3 both at line end and mid-line; `$` gives 3; `ee` on "café\nbar\n" gives 9, as in Evil. The only oracle change is motions/combining-mark-e (6 → 3), which becomes a deviation (rule "the cursor rests on a grapheme's start", kind emacs-artifact) with hand-written fixtures for `e` mid-line, `e` at line end and `$`. V3: inclusive operators extend the target to the grapheme's end.

